
         EDTALK 0.98g -- User to User chat utility for DLG Professional

                     Copyright (c) Eddy Carroll, July 1993


BETA NOTES

    NOTE: This is a BETA copy and as such, is not yet complete and may
    contain bugs. (That said, it has been fairly extensively tested over
    the past few weeks, and seems to be pretty stable.) Please report all
    bugs and enhancement suggestions to ecarroll@maths.tcd.ie. If you don't
    like big doc files, you can get away with just reading the Introduction
    and Installation sections below.


INTRODUCTION

    EdTalk is a DLG chat utility which lets any two users on the BBS chat
    to each other using a full screen display. Each user has their own
    window so they can both type simultaneously without causing confusion.
    For users without ANSI capability, EdTalk can also operate in line mode
    like more conventional chat utilities.

    I wrote EdTalk because my users had a lot of trouble with PeopleTalk,
    particularly the half-duplex aspect, and in general found it to be
    severe overkill for the simple task of letting two people chat to
    each other.


INSTALLATION

    Installing EdTalk is fairly simply. Copy the EdTalk executable into
    DLG: and the configuration file EdTalk.Config into DlgConfig:Misc (the
    defaults will suit 99% of DLG boards out there). Now add a new menu
    item to your main menu with the following executable command line:

        Dlg:EdTalk %PORT %UNAME

    Answer "No" to the questions "Chain (Y/n)?" and "Run in CLI mode (y/N)"
    and "Yes" to "Pend Messages?", and just press Return to accept defaults
    for all the other questions. That's it -- installation is done!  You can
    now go and try it out.

    You might like to setup a second menu option as well, with the following
    executable command line:

        Dlg:EdTalk -s %PORT %UNAME

    This will make EdTalk behave like DLG's Broadcast utility (send a message
    to another node) with the difference that it also lets you send a message
    directly to someone currently inside EdTalk; if you use Broadcast to send
    a message, the other user won't see the message until they have exited
    from EdTalk, which may not be for some time (hopefully :-)


USING EDTALK

    The first time you run EdTalk, it will ask you some questions to set
    up your preferences. You can simply press return for each question to
    accept the most common answers. The first question asks what sort of
    screen display you prefer. Fullscreen mode uses ANSI positioning codes
    and gives each user a separate window to type into. If your terminal
    can't support full screen, you should opt for line mode instead, which
    is similar to the Sysop chats you normally find on a BBS, with each
    user taking it in turns to type in.

    During a chat session, full screen mode will only be activated if
    both users have selected it; otherwise it will use line mode. You
    can always change your current screen mode, either from the main
    Chat menu (see below) or while in the middle of chatting with
    someone.

    The second question asks if your terminal can handle IBM graphics
    characters; this controls what character is used to form the dividing
    bar between onscreen windows. If you answer incorrectly here, you can
    always change your mind later on.

    Finally, you may be asked if you want to use your alias in chat sessions.
    If you answer no, then your real name is used. (Only users with an
    appropriate access level will be given the option to use aliases.)

    After these questions are out of the way, you will see the main EdTalk
    menu. This lists all the other users currently online (with their
    current activity string), and also some options to change your user
    options, change your availability status, and return to the main menu.
    If there are no other users online, then only the latter options are
    available.

    You can redisplay the menu by typing ?. This will update the list of
    online users and what they are doing, so you can use it to monitor
    people's activities if you like. Note that if you have hotkeys enabled
    in your user options, then EdTalk will respond immediately to each menu
    option, otherwise you'll need to press Return after typing the option
    letter.

    If there are other users on the system who are currently trying to
    chat to you, then they will be listed at the top of the menu with their
    activity string in white. Users who have set their chat preference to
    Unavailable will have their names printed in green instead of cyan, and
    trying to chat with them will simply produce a message to the effect
    that they are not available at the moment.

    Note that your user level will determine how many ports are checked to
    build the list of online users (see the section on the configuration
    file below). For example, it is possible for the Sysop to arrange that
    he/she will only appear on the chat menus of users who have a user level
    of 200 or above (i.e. co-Sysops). This can be useful if your users tend
    to pester you a lot.

    Once you've decided you want to chat with a user, you simply type the
    number listed beside their name. You will then start waiting for that
    user to respond. If the user happens to be in EdTalk at that time, they
    automatically get a message saying that you're now waiting to chat with
    with them. Otherwise, it's a good idea to send them a message to that
    effect using the page option.

    While you're waiting, an onscreen display (updated once a second or so)
    shows you what the user is doing, so you can see if they are coming to
    the chat menu or blatantly ignoring you. Typing <ESC> at this point gets
    you back to the main menu. You can also press <P> to send a message
    ("page") to the user you are waiting for. If the user is currently using
    EdTalk, the message will appear immediately on their screen, otherwise
    it will be sent using DLG:Broadcast, and appear the next time they
    reach a DLG menu prompt.

    When the other user wants to come and chat with you, they choose the
    same option you chose to run EdTalk. Instead of being shown the chat
    menu, they are immediately asked if they want to chat with you. If they
    say they don't, you get a message saying so and are brought back to the
    chat menu. Otherwise, you both go into chat mode.

    At this point, you can both type simultaneously. Whatever you type
    appears on the other user's screen and vice versa. If either you or
    the other user has set their preferred screen mode to line mode, then
    the chat will be line based, otherwise it will be full screen. In line
    mode, you should take it in turns to type, otherwise the display can
    easily get confused.

    While you are chatting, messages received from other users in EdTalk
    will be displayed on the status line at the bottom of the screen. This
    allows you to see if someone else wants to chat with you while you
    are in the middle of a conversation with someone.

    There are various keypresses available while chatting (some are only
    available in full screen mode, and some are only available in line mode).
    These are as follows:

      <CTRL-B>   Change the character used for the divider bars (between the
                 IBM bar character and a minus sign)

      <CTRL-C>   Ends the conversation (like CTRL-Z)

      <CTRL-F>   Switch from line mode to full screen mode.

      <CTRL-L>   Refresh the screen (handy in case of line noise)

      <CTRL-N>   Switch from full screen mode to line mode

      <CTRL-O>   Switches windows between wrap-around mode and scroll mode.
                 If your terminal doesn't work with DLG's full screen editor,
                 then it probably won't work with scroll mode either.

      <CTRL-W>   Clear your chat window

      <CTRL-X>   Clear to start of line (line mode only)

      <CTRL-Z>   End the conversation and return to DLG

      <ESC>      Display next help message on top line of screen

    Once either one of you ends the conversation, you will both be returned
    to the DLG Menu you called EdTalk from.


USER OPTIONS

    The User Options selection from the main menu brings you into a sub-menu
    where you can change a variety of user options. The options are as
    follows:

    A - Alias          This lets you select an alias to use in chat sessions.
                       Your alias will be used while chatting, but outside
                       of chat, your real name will appear.

    B - Message Bell   When turned on, messages received from other users
                       or other sections of the BBS will be accompanied by
                       a CTRL-G (bell), to alert you that a message has
                       arrived.

    C - Character set  This controls whether IBM or Ascii characters are used
                       to draw the screen display in full screen mode.

    Q - After chatting This controls what happens when you finish chatting
                       with someone in EdTalk. Some users like to return to
                       the EdTalk chat menu so they can chat with someone
                       else, while others prefer to leave EdTalk altogether.

    S - Screen type    This defines your preferred screen mode, full screen
                       or line mode. Even when you have selected full screen
                       mode, you may occasionally find yourself in line mode
                       if you are chatting with someone who has not also
                       turned on full screen mode.

    W - Window type    Normally, when in full screen mode, EdTalk wraps at
                       the bottom of each window and moves the cursor back to
                       the top again; this takes minimal time, but does mean
                       that it can get a little confusing as to where the
                       typing is taking place.

                       If scrolling windows are enabled, then each window
                       will scroll when the cursor reaches the bottom line.
                       This is easier to follow, but has the side effect that
                       it causes the screen to jump each time a scroll occurs.
                       In addition, not all terminals can support scrolling
                       (and in the case of PC's, may even crash completely).

    Any settings which you change are automatically saved for your next
    session in EdTalk.


USING EDTALK TO SEND MESSAGES

    As described earlier, you can run EdTalk with the -s option to make
    it behave like DLG's Broadcast utility. Using this is pretty straight-
    forward; just pick a user from the list, and then type the message to
    be sent.

    The length of the message that you can send varies -- if the user you're
    sending to is not running EdTalk, then you can send up to 70 characters,
    the standard limit imposed by Dlg:Broadcast. If the user is running
    EdTalk, then the message must be short enough to allow it to be displayed
    on a single line in the form "Joe User says: xxxxx". This is because
    there must be room for it to fit onto the message line in a full-screen
    chat message.

    The list of users to which a message can be sent is identical to the
    list the user would see on the main EdTalk chat menu. The only difference
    is that it is possible to send messages to users who have set their
    chat preferences to Unavailable, while it's not possible to chat with
    such users.


THE CONFIGURATION FILE

    The mysterious EdTalk.config file which lives in DlgConfig:Misc is
    actually just a plain text file that lists various global settings
    used by EdTalk. If you want to customise EdTalk a little, or just
    find out a bit more about how it works, read on.

    Each line of the file contains either a setting or a comment (comments
    are anything following a semicolon). The available settings are:

    Ports = Level, TR0, TR1, TR2, TR3, TL0 ...

        This setting defines all the DLG ports that EdTalk scans when it is
        displaying the list of available users on the chat menu. If a user
        is connected to a port not on this list, they will never appear
        to other users, though they themselves can start a chat session
        with other users if they so choose.

        The first item in the list is a user level from 0-255. This indicates
        what user level on your system the list corresponds to. For example,
        you might want regular callers to only be able to see ports TR0, TR1
        and TR2, while co-sysops at a higher level could also view TL0 so
        that they can see if the Sysop is around.

        The ports are listed separated by spaces and/or commas. Up to 20
        ports are supported in this version of EdTalk. The special port name
        CLI can be used to indicate that this user level can see EdTalk
        sessions running in a standard CLI Window.

        You have multiple Ports lines, one for each user level you want to
        support. If an exact match for a user level isn't found, then the
        next lowest match will be used instead (so users at level 50 will
        find themselves using the Ports line for level 10 users, rather than
        that for level 200 users). For best results, you should keep the
        Ports lines ordered in ascending order of level.

    AvailChar  = A
    OptionChar = O
    QuitChar   = M

        These three settings are all very similar; they define the letters
        used for the menu options on the chat menu. In general, the only
        one you might want to change is the QuitChar, to match whatever quit
        option you use on all your other menus. However, if you are using
        more than 9 ports with EdTalk, you might also want to change the
        AvailChar to a letter other than A, since port numbering after 9
        goes A, B, C, D, E etc.

    MinUserLevel = 1

        This setting defines the minimum user level of any users that can
        appear on the chat menu. This is useful if you have made EdTalk
        available only to users with a level >= 10, for example, since it
        prevents new users who can't use EdTalk themselves from appearing
        on the chat menu for other users to try and talk to. If you leave
        out this command, then it defaults to 1.

    MinAliasLevel = 255

        This setting defines the minimum user level a user must have in
        order to use an alias instead of their real name when chatting.
        By default, it is set to allow only the Sysop to use an Alias
        (for example, "Eddy Carroll (Sysop)") but you might prefer to
        let users choose their own alias, in which case you would reduce
        the value to 10 or even 1.

    StayInEdTalk = 1

        This setting controls what each new user's default action will be
        when they finish chatting with someone. If 1 (the default) then
        the user will be returned to the EdTalk chat menu. If 0, then they
        will be returned to the main BBS menu. If you are running a 1 or 2
        line BBS system, returning to the main BBS menu is usually the
        preferred action (since there's nobody else to chat with) but
        multi-line systems will probably prefer to leave it at 1.

        In any case, whatever default you choose, each user can override
        it in their own personal options.

    If you need more flexibility over configuration, you can use the -c
    option on EdTalk's command line to specify a configuration file other
    than DlgConfig:Misc/EdTalk.Config. For example, you could use a command
    line like this:

        EdTalk -c DlgConfig:Misc/EdTalk.Config-%LEVEL %PORT %UNAME

    and then setup separate configuration files for each user level you
    support (EdTalk.Config-10, EdTalk.Config-200, etc.) You can also use
    this method to use different configuration files for the standard
    EdTalk and EdTalk with the -s option (to send a message).


RUNNING EDTALK FROM THE CLI

    If you're a Sysop, you may want to run EdTalk from the CLI rather
    than having to logon just to chat to someone. This is fairly easily
    achieved: just type:

        EDTALK CLI Your_Name

    You don't have to use your real name; you can use "Sysop" or "God" or
    anything else that takes your fancy. If you want to have ANSI colour in
    your CLI window, then add the keyword ON to the end of the display line;
    by default, it is turned off.

    When EdTalk starts, it will be just the same as running it online from
    an unlisted port. By default, the CLI Availability setting will usually
    be Available, since if you don't want people to see your CLI sessions,
    you won't have listed CLI on any of the Ports lines in the config file.
    Note that CLI sessions run as if they were user level 255 (i.e. Sysop).

    Ideally, you should always have at least one Ports line in your config
    file that includes CLI, even if it's only for level 255. This allows you
    to run EdTalk from two separate CLI's and then let the two of them chat
    to each other, without anyone being logged into DLG at all. You might
    find this handy if you want to experiment with EdTalk and you find
    yourself a bit short of users.

    I recommend you setup the following two Shell aliases for convenience:

        alias edtalk "Dlg:EdTalk CLI My_User_Name"
        alias edsend "Dlg:EdTalk -s CLI My_User_Name"

    Include the ANSI option COLOR at the end of each command line if you like.
    Then just type EdTalk to chat with any users currently online (or even
    to list those who are around), and EdSend to send a message without
    chatting.


DISTRIBUTION

    Edtalk is freely distributable software, and requires no payment of
    shareware fees. If you use it and like it, and happen to have written
    some nice DLG Shareware yourself, registered copies of such items are
    very welcome and can be sent to me using any of the methods listed in
    the AUTHOR section below.

    If you hack the source code yourself to add new features, please
    forward such changes back to me so I can consider adding them into
    the main version. If you release a modified version of EdTalk, you
    must abide by the following:

      - Retain all original documentation and copyright notices
      - Include full details of any changes you have made to EdTalk
      - Modify the ShowMenu() function to display your own name as
        well as mine
      - Name your new version EdTalk-Xyzzy where Xyzzy is some prefix
        you decide on to distinguish it from the original version
        (EdTalk-Xeno, EdTalk-CNet, or something along those lines.)

    Note: Source is not included with the beta versions, until it reaches
    a stable state.


